{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And you shall see, etc. what it is.</b> Meaning: for there is a land whose climate and air are pure and healthful and naturally balanced, and there is one whose air is turbid and miasmic and opposed to the natural temperament. Also, there is a land that is full of springs and its waters are clean and they produce a good temperament in a person and sustain the faculties, and there is the opposite. And these are things that exist in the land on its own account without an intermediary cause. Corresponding to these things He said: ״וּרְאִיתֶם אֶת הָאָרֶץ <b>מַה הִיא</b>״ — regarding its physical constitution, whether it is in the category of the desirable or the opposite. And on the other hand, this investigation has aspects some of which are discernible by way of their passing through it, and there are things that require testing — as it says, whether it sustains human nature, whether its waters are fertile and increase; these things are not recognizable by way of passing through. Therefore He commanded them <b>and the people, etc.</b> — meaning, from their actions the powers will be known; from the people dwelling in it the power of the land will be revealed, whether the people are strong or weak. And since one might say there is no test from the strength of the people, because there is room to depend on something else — namely that they guard themselves from marital relations — therefore He said <b>whether it is few or whether it is many</b>; in this too it will be revealed whether its waters are fertile and increase." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to raise the question of why Scripture changed the order that it adopted in all the gradations, in which it preceded with the favorable part and afterward mentioned the unfavorable part: ״הֶחָזָק הוּא״ (whether it is strong) and afterward ״הֲרָפֶה״ (or weak); ״הַטּוֹבָה הִיא״ (whether it is good) — it preceded with ״good״ and afterward ״אִם רָעָה״ (or bad); and also the test of ״הֶחָזָק הוּא״ is the favorable part, to know the goodness of the land that raises strong people; and likewise in the last statement it preceded by saying ״הַשְּׁמֵנָה הִיא״ (whether it is fat) and afterward ״אִם רָזָה״ (or lean); and likewise ״הֲיֵשׁ בָּהּ עֵץ״ (does it have trees) and afterward He said ״אִם אַיִן״ (or not). Why then, when He mentioned the fewness and the multitude, did He precede by saying ״הַמְעַט הוּא אִם רָב״ (whether they are few or whether many)? It would seem to say that even in this test He preceded with the favorable part — namely, that if they are few it is better for Israel — but this is not so, for He would not remain in suspense regarding a good thing when He knows its definitive negation?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention is along this line: <b>whether they are few, etc.</b> — meaning, it reverts to the statement ״הֶחָזָק הוּא״ (whether it is strong), etc. If the strength of the people is a great thing — that they are few just like those who are many among them as in the rest of the world — accordingly He preceded with the more distinguished part, in praise of the land, that there is none like it in raising strong people in all the lands. Or, on the side of recognizing the miracle as well, as I have written. And along this line it is not difficult why He interrupted the topic with the statement ״וְאֶת הָעָם״ (and the people), etc., in the midst of matters concerning the land — for afterward He says ״וּמָה הָאָרֶץ״ (and what is the land) and completes it; and according to our words, the verse is speaking about matters of the land."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:29.600932536Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}